## Solution

**Setup.** Place the square in the coordinate plane with $A=(0,0)$, $B=(2,0)$, $C=(2,2)$, $D=(0,2)$. Then
$$P=(0.5,0),\qquad Q=(1.5,0),\qquad |PQ|=1,\quad M=(1,0)\text{ (midpoint)}.$$
The camera $E$ lies on the perimeter of $ABCD$; we maximize $\angle PEQ$.

**Key principle.** For a fixed chord $PQ$, the locus of points $E$ with $\angle PEQ=\theta$ (constant) is a pair of circular arcs through $P,Q$. Larger $\theta$ corresponds to a *smaller* circle (since the chord length $|PQ|=2R\sin\theta$ is fixed, so $R=|PQ|/(2\sin\theta)$ decreases as $\theta$ increases). Hence $\angle PEQ$ is maximized on the perimeter at the point lying on the *smallest* circle through $P,Q$ that still touches the perimeter.

**Candidates by symmetry.** Since $P,Q$ are symmetric about $x=1$, it suffices to examine the right edge $BC$ (the left edge $DA$ is symmetric) and the top edge $CD$.

*Top edge $CD$:* $E=(s,2)$. By symmetry the best point is $s=1$, i.e. $E=(1,2)$. Then
$$\cos\angle PEQ=\frac{(-\tfrac12,-2)\cdot(\tfrac12,-2)}{\tfrac{17}{4}}=\frac{-\tfrac14+4}{\tfrac{17}{4}}=\frac{15}{17},\qquad \angle PEQ=\arccos\tfrac{15}{17}\approx 28.07^\circ.$$

*Right edge $BC$:* write $E=(2,t)$, $t\in[0,2]$. Then
$$\overrightarrow{EP}=(-1.5,-t),\quad \overrightarrow{EQ}=(-0.5,-t),$$
$$\cos\theta(t)=\frac{0.75+t^{2}}{\sqrt{(2.25+t^{2})(0.25+t^{2})}}.$$
Set $u=t^{2}$ and minimize $\cos\theta$, equivalently minimize
$$f(u)=\frac{(0.75+u)^{2}}{(2.25+u)(0.25+u)}.$$
Differentiating,
$$f'(u)\propto u^{2}-0.5625,$$
so the critical point is $u=0.75$, i.e. $t=\sqrt{3}/2$. At this point
$$\cos\theta=\sqrt{f(0.75)}=\sqrt{\frac{(1.5)^{2}}{3\cdot 1}}=\sqrt{\frac{3}{4}}=\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\qquad \theta=30^\circ.$$
The endpoints give $0^\circ$ (at $B$, collinear) and $\arccos(15/17)\approx 28.07^\circ$ (at $C=(2,2)$), both smaller. So the maximum on $BC$ is $30^\circ$ at $E=(2,\sqrt3/2)$.

**This is the global maximum.** The angle $30^\circ$ corresponds to a circle of radius $R=|PQ|/(2\sin30^\circ)=1$ through $P,Q$. Its center lies on $x=1$ at $(1,\sqrt3/2)$ (taking the upper circle), and the rightmost point of this circle is $(2,\sqrt3/2)=E$, which is exactly where the circle is *tangent* to the edge $BC$. Any circle through $P,Q$ giving a larger angle would have radius $<1$ and would lie strictly inside the square, touching no perimeter point. Hence no perimeter point yields $\angle PEQ>30^\circ$. By symmetry, $E=(0,\sqrt3/2)$ on $DA$ is the other maximizer; both give the same triangle area.

**Computing the area.** Take $E=(2,\sqrt3/2)$, $P=(0.5,0)$, $Q=(1.5,0)$. The base $PQ$ lies on the line $y=0$ with length $|PQ|=1$. The height of $E$ above this line is the $y$-coordinate of $E$, namely $\sqrt3/2$. Therefore
$$[\triangle PEQ]=\tfrac12\cdot |PQ|\cdot\text{height}=\tfrac12\cdot 1\cdot \frac{\sqrt3}{2}=\frac{\sqrt3}{4}.$$

**Verification of the angle at the optimum.**
$$|EP|^{2}=1.5^{2}+\tfrac34=3,\quad |EQ|^{2}=0.5^{2}+\tfrac34=1,\quad \overrightarrow{EP}\cdot\overrightarrow{EQ}=0.75+\tfrac34=1.5,$$
$$\cos\angle PEQ=\frac{1.5}{\sqrt3\cdot 1}=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\implies \angle PEQ=30^\circ.\ \checkmark$$

The area of the triangular region $PEQ$ at the optimal camera position is

$$\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}}.$$

